s down you'll have live copies of  your data in another zone within the  same region and you can access it there  then people sort of moved to  multi-regional multi-regional is where  you have resources that are holding your  data and they're not just spread out  within a region they're actually spread  out through what's called a multi  regional area so there are three multi  regional areas in Google cloud platform  there's a North American or a US region  there's an Emir region and an  asia-pacific region so this gives you  even higher levels of availability if  the western region goes down you can  still read your data from the central or  the eastern region so you can see  actually that the availability is better  with multi regional as opposed to  regional you also get a performance  improvement in that because your data is  in multiple locations throughout the  United States it's more likely that any  given user will be closer to that data  on the right hand side then you have  near line and cold line storage and  these are intended to hold data at  reduced costs but it's data that you  don't access as often so basically what  happens is if you put something in near  line Google essentially puts it  someplace that it's less expensive for  Google to store but it's more expensive  for Google to serve so what they do is  they cut the price for storage but then  they make you pay for accessing it you  don't pay a performance penalty you  still have access times in tens of  milliseconds and you still have high  throughput but they have to go through  more contortions to serve the data at  that speed therefore while it cost less  to store it actually cost more to access  so it's really good if you access this  data infrequently so at the top here we  so this is intended for data that is  frequently accessed across regions with  inner region access less than once a  month or access less than once a year  durability is a lot of nines  availability the worst case scenario is  1% of the time you might not be able to  access your near line or cold line data  at a given point  in time and it's three 9s for regional  and it's three nines and a 5/4  multi-regional you use the same api's or  the same GUI or the same command-line  utilities for getting data in and out of  all of these storage classes you have  the same latency for reads the  difference here is on cost so at the top  end the top shelf multi-regional storage  class you pay 2.6 cents per gigabyte per  month for regional you get a discount  you pay 2 cents per gigabyte per month  Nearline you pay half that you pay one  cent per gigabyte per month what's  interesting though is you pay roughly 1  cent per gigabyte to read the data off  the disk so if you read the data once a  month to you about breakeven if you read  the data once every three months you're  clearly gonna come out ahead vs.  regional if you read the data three  times a month you would actually pay  more for Nearline than you did for  regional and then cold line you pay  seven tenths of a cent per gigabyte per  month but you pay a higher access charge  now storage when it comes to the cost  entailed in Google Cloud Storage you  paid this ongoing storage fee then for  an airline and cold line you pay to  access or read the data off the disk and  then you also pay for what's called  Network egress so that is any data that  leaves the region where the data is  stored you end up having to pay and it  starts at about the lowest price is  about 8 cents per terabyte transfer now  again if you're using a regional bucket  and your server is in the West Region  and it's reading and writing to a cloud  bucket that's in the West Region it  never leaves the West Region data  centers therefore you don't pay network  egress but for instance with that  learning management system with a little  video lesson I'm paying 20 cents per or  I'm sorry 2 cents per gigabyte per month  to store it and then every time somebody  loads that